		<meta property="schema:genre">Fiction</meta>
		<meta property="schema:abstract">To quit smoking, a man writes his memoirs.</meta>
		<dc:description>
			&lt;p&gt;Zeno Cosini is a businessman in Trieste. Seeking to quit smoking, he starts treatment with a psychoanalyst, who asks him to write his memoirs, believing this will lead to a cure.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;Zeno’s memoirs don’t follow a linear structure, and are instead organized by topic; it soon becomes apparent that he’s an unreliable narrator. He speaks of cigarettes, his studies, his marriage, and his business. Interlaced with his recollections are his ideas about society and the modern world, especially psychoanalysis, a then-new field of science. The character is likely partially based on &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/italo-svevo"&gt;Svevo&lt;/a&gt; himself, who also lived in Trieste and was a heavy smoker. &lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;&lt;i&gt;Confessions of Zeno&lt;/i&gt; is Italo Svevo’s third and final novel. His previous novels were ignored by critics and the public alike, and this one would have also remained obscure if not for Svevo’s friend and former English tutor, &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/james-joyce"&gt;James Joyce&lt;/a&gt;. Joyce pushed for the novel to be translated into French; the translation received high praises from critics, leading to Svevo becoming a respected part of the modernist milieu. &lt;/p&gt;
		</dc:description>
